	// to say, must it carry the green-thread stack guard despite being the compiler's own emitted code. FALSE
	// for the runtime's own scaffolding, and false for every parsed function a `runtime/` file declares — which
	// IS asked, because `isRuntimeFunction` answers `true` for a tier body and `skipsGreenThreadStackGuard`
	// then reads this field. TRUE for two families. The per-type CASCADES — `__destruct_<T>`,
	// `__clone_<T>`, `__share_<T>`, their dictionary twins and the one-argument element thunks — recurse once
	// per LEVEL of the user's data graph, so their frame count is a property of the program's types and nothing
	// in the runtime bounds it. `__module_init` and `__maxon_global_cleanup` are the first and last frames on
	// `main`'s green thread in a program with green threads (`BacktraceFormat.MainThreadEntryName`), where no
	// guarded caller has projected any room for them.
	//
	// ⛔ **IT EXISTS BECAUSE PROVENANCE AND STACK BEHAVIOUR ARE DIFFERENT QUESTIONS.**
	// `TargetPrinter.isRuntimeFunction` (the `runtime/` tier, or the `mrt_`/`__` band with no source file)
	// withholds the
	// green-thread stack guard, on the premise that *"the scheduler core must never trigger the grower, and
	// their kernel calls are already shimmed onto the system stack"*. Neither family satisfies it: both run on
	// a green thread, with real frames — which is precisely what the guard is for. MEASURED on x64-windows: a 128-deep nested-struct
	// chain deep-moved to a service and released by `ServiceBehaviour.dropUnconsumedPayloads` SIGSEGVs 8/8 through
	// 128 unguarded `__destruct_` frames on a green-thread stack.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT IS NOT EVERY `__`-BAND FRAME A GREEN THREAD RUNS, and the flat container walkers
	// (`__managed_*`, `__list_*`, `__drop_existential`) DELIBERATELY stay exempt.** They are not
	// recursive: at most a couple of them sit between one cascade level's checkpoint and the next,
	// which is exactly the free-ride `GtStackGuardMarginBytes` is sized to cover (see
	// `gtStackGuardMargin`, whose second term names them). The families above are the ones the margin
	// cannot cover, because their count grows with the program's data.
	//
